      "text": "To find the smallest number divisible by integers 1 through 9, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of these numbers. The LCM of a set of integers is the smallest positive integer that is divisible by each of them.\n\nLet's start by finding the prime factorization of each number from 1 to 9:\n\n1. \\(1\\) (trivially divisible by any number)\n2. \\(2 = 2\\)\n3. \\(3 = 3\\)\n4. \\(4 = 2^2\\)\n5. \\(5 = 5\\)\n6. \\(6 = 2 \\cdot 3\\)\n7. \\(7 = 7\\)\n8. \\(8 = 2^3\\)\n9. \\(9 = 3^2\\)\n\nNow, we identify the highest power of each prime number that appears in the factorizations:\n\n- The highest power of \\(2\\) is \\(2^3\\) (from 8).\n- The highest power of \\(3\\) is \\(3^2\\) (from 9).\n- The highest power of \\(5\\) is \\(5^1\\) (from 5).\n- The highest power of \\(7\\) is \\(7^1\\) (from 7).\n\nTo find the LCM, we take the product of these highest powers:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 5^1 \\times 7^1\n\\]\n\nCalculating this product step-by-step:\n\n\\[\n2^3 = 8\n\\]\n\\[\n3^2 = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n8 \\times 9 = 72\n\\]\n\\[\n72 \\times 5 = 360\n\\]\n\\[\n360 \\times 7 = 2520\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest number divisible by integers 1 through 9 is \\(\\boxed{2520}\\).",
